        "title": "Light years ahead",
        "subtitle": "Cheap solar panels could be the answer to Uganda's power shortage, but they are proving hard to sell.",
        "text": "Uganda may be one of the poorest countries in the world, but it has been blessed with an almost perfect climate. With so much sunshine to spare, one Ugandan electrician believes he has the solution to the country's power shortage: low-tech solar panels that can run anything from a radio to a mobile phone.\n\nThe electrician, Fred Kajubi, is a member of the Uganda Change Agents Association, an organisation that helps local people learn skills that can make a difference to their lives and to their communities. Its members, known as Change Agents, run credit unions, set up self-help groups in villages and become active in local politics, and in Mr Kajubi's case, they promote the use of solar power. He has set up his own small company, Sunshine Solutions, which offers customers a solar panel to meet their every need.\n\nThe materials for the panels come from BioDesign, a British company set up five years ago by a retired inventor, Graham Knight. After watching a television programme about the invention of a clockwork radio, Mr Knight decided that in some parts of the world solar power would be a more effective energy source for radios and similar everyday equipment. He started a firm to make the components for low-cost solar panels for use in Africa and South America. These are sent out as kits, together with instructions on how to put them together. Graham Knight's panels use amorphous silicon, and they are ten times cheaper than the crystalline silicon panels more commonly used for large-scale solar power production. As a result, Sunshine Solutions can sell solar panels that are far cheaper than the ones in the shops.\n\nOnly a small minority of Ugandans have access to mains electricity at present, so most families rely on batteries to power their radios and on kerosene lamps to light their homes. Yet for just 15,000 shillings ($8.50), the same as two months' supply of batteries, one of the panels sold by Sunshine Solutions can run a radio for several years. Even so, the company is finding it a struggle to persuade people to invest in its solar panels. Although the panels work out cheaper than batteries in the long term, the initial cost is more than many people can afford.\n\nUganda plans to bring power to poor villages over the next five years by building a big dam on the Nile. But even if these ambitious plans for rural electrification succeed, some sections of the population will still be unable to afford to connect to the national grid. \"The experience of the last couple of decades in developing countries is that ambitious schemes are not effective in getting power to the poorest people,\" says Andrew Simms, an expert from the New Economics Foundation in London. \"Small-scale enterprises have a better track record at getting energy to the people who need it. Better still, solar and other renewable energy sources allow countries to avoid the effects of pollution caused by heavy reliance on fossil fuels,\" Mr Simms says.\n\nEven the World Bank, which is often criticised for being obsessed with large-scale power projects, recognises that there is a place for solar power. According to a World Bank representative, solar power can be an effective complement to grid-based electricity, which is often too costly for sparsely settled and remote areas.\n\nBut even cheap technology is hard to sell in a country where half the population lives below the poverty line and there are few effective channels for marketing and distribution. Mr Simms believes that the only way to spread solar energy more widely is government subsidies, because the initial costs of solar power panels are beyond most household budgets.\n\nMeanwhile, Mr Kajubi is pinning his hopes on the spread of micro-credit schemes that will lend money to families to help them raise the cash for his products. His company has not made a profit yet, although he says sales are picking up. He is planning another trip into the countryside to demonstrate his solar panels, as well as a new solar cooker. Asked whether he ever feels downhearted, he points to the motto on his workshop wall: \"Never give up,\" it says."
